     Ah, *this* explains why my God always seems to get angry 
with me when something good happens: I save and backup my 
game so as to work around system crashes!  (And sometimes I 
restart them and switch to discovery mode, as well, although I 
like to keep my score list full of "kosher" games.)  If it's a 
full moon (I hope only on a full moon) my luck (normally 0 
until and unless I find a luckstone) decreases to -1, which, 
if I interpret the code right, *guarantees* an angry God.
     On the up side, all those things I thought were 
restricting my game such as wielding Excalibur, having speed 
boots, robbing shops and so forth seem to have no effect on my 
food consumption or luck whatsoever.  All I need to do is 
verify that this only happens with full moons, find a 
"neutral" date, and set up an alias (I'm on a PC, but I'm 
using 4DOS, which allows aliases, just to clear up any 
confusion as to how I can set the system date) to set it to 
such a date before playing.  Of course, anyone else using this 
tactic should probably know that dates before the "last build 
time" as a time stamp on your save file will result in 
deletion (not just failure; deletion) of the file when you 
attempt to restore.  The game makes no such check when the 
game is saved, however, which means this bug (or, at least, 
badly-made feature, IMO), which should probably only exist on 
the PC version of the game, is very difficult to discover the 
cause of when it hits one.  To the credit of the PC branch of 
the dev team, Kevin Smolkowski has informed me that he plans 
to fix this in 3.1 by writing the build date for the game
into the save file and using this instead of the time stamp to 
determine whether the save file is out of date.
     Sheesh!  What a minus, that full-moon bug.  Still a great 
game, though.

